.TH MKSCRIPT 1 "2026-08-11" "mkscript 1.4.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkscript \- create Bash, Terraform, and Ansible starter files safely
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkscript
[\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-files\fR [\fIdepth\fR]]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.br
.B mkscript
[\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-files\fR]
\fIname\fR
[\fIname\fR ...]
.br
.B mkscript
[\fB\-t\fR|\fB\-\-template\fR \fItemplate\fR]
[\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-strict\fR]
[\fB\-g\fR|\fB\-\-global\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR]
[\fB\-r\fR]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.I path
.br
.B mkscript
[\fB\-g\fR|\fB\-\-global\fR]
\fB\-mv\fR
.I source
.I target
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkscript
creates a new file at the requested path from a Bash, Terraform, or Ansible starter template.
The default template is Bash.
For Bash files,
.B mkscript
writes the standard shebang followed by a small comment header
with the script name, a blank description line, the local creation date, and the
creator login name:
.PP
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Script: hello-world
# Description:
# Created: YYYY-MM-DD
# Creator: login-user
.EE
.PP
For Terraform and Ansible files,
.B mkscript
writes the same metadata header with
.B File
in place of
.BR Script .
.PP
When
.B \-s
or
.B \-\-strict
is supplied,
.B mkscript
also writes:
.PP
.EX
set -euo pipefail
.EE
.PP
When
.B \-g
or
.B \-\-global
is supplied,
.B mkscript
creates a symlink in the detected user bin directory
using the script basename.
If the requested path does not exist yet,
.B mkscript
creates the new Bash script and then creates the symlink.
If the requested path already exists locally,
.B mkscript
asks for confirmation and creates only the symlink without rewriting the local file.
.PP
.B \-s ,
.BR \-\-strict ,
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-\-global ,
.BR \-c ,
and
.B \-r
are supported only for the Bash template.
.PP
.B \-c
checks whether the expected global symlink already exists and prints its path when it does.
.PP
.B \-r
removes the expected global symlink after confirmation.
.PP
.B \-f
or
.B \-\-files
scans the current directory tree and prints a table of matching local files.
.I depth
may be supplied as a single digit from 0 through 9 to limit how many subfolder levels are scanned.
A file is listed when it ends with
.BR .sh ,
is executable, or is the local target of a managed global symlink.
.PP
When
.B \-f
or
.B \-\-files
receives one to nine names from arguments or newline-separated standard input,
.B mkscript
switches to lookup mode and prints the resolved location and parent directory for each requested name.
Lookup names may be exact names or quoted shell-style glob patterns such as
.BR 'install-wifi*' .
Quote pattern arguments so the shell does not expand them before
.B mkscript
receives them.
Lookup mode checks an exact path first, then exact or pattern command matches on
.BR PATH ,
then current-tree and wider filename matches.
Lookup mode returns the first resolved match for each query.
.PP
.B \-mv
moves an existing local Bash script to a new path, preserves its permission mode,
and recreates the global shortcut when the source was linked already or when
.B \-g
is supplied.
.PP
Existing files, symlinks, and directories are never overwritten.
.PP
On Linux,
.B mkscript
uses
.BR ~/.local/bin .
On macOS, it prefers a personal local bin directory already on
.B PATH
before falling back to
.BR ~/.local/bin .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-template
Select the file template.
Supported values are
.BR bash ,
.BR terraform ,
and
.BR ansible .
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-files
List matching local files under the current directory tree in a table with
path, kind, executable state, and global-link state.
When supplied, the optional
.I depth
value must be a single digit from 0 through 9, where 0 means the current directory only.
When one to nine
.I name
arguments are supplied, or when newline-separated names are piped to standard input,
lookup mode prints a table with query, found state, result type, resolved location, and parent directory.
Quoted shell-style glob patterns such as
.BR 'install-wifi*'
are supported in lookup mode.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-strict
Add
.B set -euo pipefail
after the shebang for the Bash template.
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-global
Create a symlink in the detected user bin directory
that points to the new Bash script, or if the requested local path already exists,
confirm and link that existing local Bash file instead of rewriting it.
.TP
.B \-c
Check whether the expected global Bash symlink exists and print its path when it does.
.TP
.B \-r
Remove the expected global Bash symlink after confirmation.
.TP
.B \-mv
Move an existing local Bash script to a new path, preserve its permission mode,
and recreate the global shortcut when appropriate.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display usage information and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display the program version and exit successfully.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 64
Command-line usage error.
.TP
.B 1
The requested link was not present for check or removal mode, or one or more lookup names were not found.
.TP
.B 73
The target script could not be created safely.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create a simple script in the current directory:
.EX
mkscript hello-world
.EE
.TP
Create a Terraform starter file:
.EX
mkscript --template terraform main.tf
.EE
.TP
Create an Ansible playbook starter file:
.EX
mkscript site.yml --template ansible
.EE
.TP
Create a strict-mode script:
.EX
mkscript --strict deploy.sh
.EE
.TP
Create a strict-mode script with a global shortcut:
.EX
mkscript -g deploy.sh -s
.EE
.TP
List matching files under the current tree:
.EX
mkscript -f
.EE
.TP
List matching files only in the current directory:
.EX
mkscript -f 0
.EE
.TP
List matching files up to one subfolder deep:
.EX
mkscript --files 1
.EE
.TP
Look up one or more names and print the resolved location plus its parent directory:
.EX
mkscript -f bash README.md
.EE
.TP
Look up a command or filename with a quoted glob pattern:
.EX
mkscript -f 'install-wifi*'
.EE
.TP
Look up newline-separated names from standard input:
.EX
printf "%s\\n" bash README.md | mkscript -f
.EE
.TP
Move an existing Bash script and recreate its global shortcut if needed:
.EX
mkscript -mv test deploy
.EE
.TP
Link an existing local file into the global bin directory:
.EX
mkscript test.sh -g
.EE
.TP
Check a global link:
.EX
mkscript test -c
.EE
.TP
Remove a global link:
.EX
mkscript -r test
.EE
